<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Hereti</h1><p class="st-meta">850 CE – 1010 CE (approx.) · capital Shaki</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Christian principality between Georgia and Caucasian Albania whose Queen Dinar is remembered for bringing it into the Georgian Orthodox church.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hereti lay along the Alazani and Iori rivers and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan">Shaki</a>, a borderland between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kartli</a> and the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania</a>. In the 9th century, as Arab power waned, a local dynasty with ties to the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mihranid-principality-of-albania">Mihranid Principality of Albania</a> established its own rule, sometimes called the kings of the Rans.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its population mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and Caucasian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>, and its church long followed the anti-Chalcedonian Armenian tradition. In the 10th century Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinar">Dinar</a>, of Georgian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagratid</a> descent, converted the principality to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodoxy</a>. Its rulers fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-tbilisi">Emirate of Tbilisi</a> and competed with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-kakheti">Kakheti</a>, controlling farmland and routes to the Caspian.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hereti was absorbed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-kakheti">Principality of Kakheti</a> in the late 10th century, and around 1010 the two formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kakheti-hereti">Kingdom of Kakheti-Hereti</a>. The name survived as a province of the Georgian kingdom.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-hereti.jpg" alt="Principality of Hereti" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: Don-kun · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caucasus_850_map_de.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>850 CE – 1010 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shaki</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dinar, Ishkhanik</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian; Armenian; Caucasian Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hereti</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=930&amp;focus=principality-of-hereti" aria-label="Principality of Hereti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5833 708 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Hereti lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6153 964L6106 941"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6106 941h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6153 964h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6153 964h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Shaki. <a href="/atlas?year=930&amp;focus=principality-of-hereti">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
